1
00:00:01,050 --> 00:00:04,380
A polar bear stands on a
ridge of ice above the shore.

2
00:00:05,030 --> 00:00:08,560
Below it, a walrus lies
asleep on the stones.

3
00:00:09,694 --> 00:00:13,014
The bear lifts a heavy block
of ice in its front paws.

4
00:00:14,025 --> 00:00:17,905
It takes aim, and throws
it at the walrus's head.

5
00:00:18,745 --> 00:00:22,095
Then it waits to see whether the
giant below will move again.

6
00:00:24,170 --> 00:00:28,145
For more than a century, Inuit
hunters in Greenland and the Canadian

7
00:00:28,195 --> 00:00:30,740
Arctic have described
scenes like this one.

8
00:00:31,350 --> 00:00:33,703
Hunters who knew the
ice better than anyone

9
00:00:33,753 --> 00:00:36,100
said they had seen it with their own eyes.

10
00:00:37,099 --> 00:00:42,299
And for just as long, many scientists
treated those stories as legends.

11
00:00:43,089 --> 00:00:46,479
Nobody had filmed it, and
nobody had measured it.

12
00:00:47,568 --> 00:00:48,998
Because it makes no sense.

13
00:00:52,121 --> 00:00:55,391
The polar bear is the largest
predator that lives on land.

14
00:00:56,151 --> 00:00:58,601
It can kill a seal with a single blow.

15
00:00:59,361 --> 00:01:03,893
Its paws are as wide as dinner plates,
and its sense of smell can find a

16
00:01:03,943 --> 00:01:05,661
seal under a metre of snow.

17
00:01:06,729 --> 00:01:10,599
So why would the most powerful hunter
in the Arctic ever need a weapon?

18
00:01:13,724 --> 00:01:16,114
The answer is lying on that beach.

19
00:01:16,774 --> 00:01:18,704
And it weighs more than a tonne.

20
00:01:26,254 --> 00:01:31,304
To understand the problem, look
at the two animals side by side.

21
00:01:32,014 --> 00:01:35,314
On paper, the bear should win every time.

22
00:01:36,399 --> 00:01:40,579
A large male polar bear can weigh
around six hundred kilograms.

23
00:01:41,339 --> 00:01:44,949
A large male walrus can weigh
more than twice as much.

24
00:01:46,008 --> 00:01:47,648
A walrus is not a seal.

25
00:01:48,418 --> 00:01:50,228
It is closer to a fortress.

26
00:01:50,988 --> 00:01:53,348
Even its body is built for conflict.

27
00:01:54,453 --> 00:01:58,343
Its skin can be several centimetres
thick on the neck and shoulders.

28
00:01:59,003 --> 00:02:04,163
Under the skin lies a deep layer of
blubber, and under that, heavy muscle.

29
00:02:05,187 --> 00:02:09,337
Claws and teeth that open a seal
in seconds barely get through.

30
00:02:10,127 --> 00:02:14,249
The thickest skin covers exactly the
places where rival males strike each

31
00:02:14,299 --> 00:02:17,157
other with their tusks
during the breeding season.

32
00:02:18,120 --> 00:02:19,770
And then there are the tusks.

33
00:02:20,530 --> 00:02:24,510
They are canine teeth that never stop
growing, and in a big male they can

34
00:02:24,560 --> 00:02:26,050
approach a metre in length.

35
00:02:27,147 --> 00:02:32,787
A walrus uses them to haul itself out
of the water, to fight its rivals,

36
00:02:32,837 --> 00:02:34,497
and to defend itself.

37
00:02:35,566 --> 00:02:39,936
For a bear, attacking an adult
walrus head on is not hunting.

38
00:02:40,596 --> 00:02:42,556
It is a gamble with its own life.

39
00:02:49,221 --> 00:02:53,580
Pacific walruses spend much of the
year on the drifting sea ice of the

40
00:02:53,630 --> 00:02:55,141
Bering and Chukchi seas.

41
00:02:55,931 --> 00:02:59,681
They follow the ice as it grows
in winter and shrinks in summer.

42
00:03:00,723 --> 00:03:02,403
The ice is their raft.

43
00:03:03,043 --> 00:03:08,001
They rest on it between dives, give
birth on it, and ride it north as it

44
00:03:08,051 --> 00:03:09,303
melts each summer.

45
00:03:10,262 --> 00:03:14,864
Below the ice lies one of the richest
seafloors on Earth: a shallow

46
00:03:14,914 --> 00:03:18,732
continental shelf covered
with clams, worms and snails.

47
00:03:19,746 --> 00:03:25,356
There, walruses gather by the thousands,
pressed together, tusks resting

48
00:03:25,406 --> 00:03:27,136
on each other's backs.

49
00:03:27,886 --> 00:03:31,048
On a single floe, hundreds of animals can

50
00:03:31,098 --> 00:03:34,466
lie side by side,
steaming in the cold air.

51
00:03:35,486 --> 00:03:39,086
For a walrus, a crowd means safety.

52
00:03:39,846 --> 00:03:44,946
Every animal is another pair of
eyes, and another pair of tusks.

53
00:03:45,955 --> 00:03:49,015
For a polar bear, a crowd is a wall.

54
00:03:49,635 --> 00:03:53,635
And yet, every year, some
bears try to break through it.

55
00:03:56,704 --> 00:04:01,571
Most of the time, polar bears hunt
seals, waiting beside a breathing hole

56
00:04:01,621 --> 00:04:04,854
in the ice for hours until
one comes up for air.

57
00:04:05,594 --> 00:04:08,233
Walruses are something else entirely:

58
00:04:08,283 --> 00:04:11,044
bigger, rarer, and far more dangerous.

59
00:04:16,595 --> 00:04:22,584
In July 2015, on a beach in Svalbard,
a photographer filmed a hungry polar

60
00:04:22,634 --> 00:04:27,285
bear and her cub approaching
a herd of sleeping walruses.

61
00:04:28,366 --> 00:04:29,856
The bear did not charge.

62
00:04:30,576 --> 00:04:35,136
She walked slowly among them,
sniffing one walrus after another.

63
00:04:35,836 --> 00:04:38,196
Most of the walruses barely moved.

64
00:04:39,121 --> 00:04:44,263
Scientists who studied the footage
suggested she was testing them, looking

65
00:04:44,313 --> 00:04:47,471
for one that was weak,
sick, or already dead.

66
00:04:48,201 --> 00:04:51,961
A healthy adult walrus was
simply too dangerous to attack.

67
00:04:53,015 --> 00:04:55,005
Then she touched one with her paw.

68
00:04:56,091 --> 00:04:57,501
The walrus woke up.

69
00:04:58,131 --> 00:05:00,031
And it was the bear that backed away.

70
00:05:00,801 --> 00:05:04,524
A predator weighing hundreds of
kilograms had retreated from an animal

71
00:05:04,574 --> 00:05:06,191
that had only lifted its head.

72
00:05:09,253 --> 00:05:13,993
Without a meal, experts feared that
the cub would not survive for long.

73
00:05:20,555 --> 00:05:25,463
To see what really happens between
the two species, scientists went to

74
00:05:25,513 --> 00:05:28,205
Wrangel Island, in the Russian Arctic.

75
00:05:28,955 --> 00:05:33,627
The island is a nature reserve, sometimes
called the maternity ward of the

76
00:05:33,677 --> 00:05:37,315
polar bear, because so many
females dig their dens there.

77
00:05:38,386 --> 00:05:43,898
In the autumn of 1990, the Russian
biologists Nikita Ovsyanikov and

78
00:05:43,948 --> 00:05:49,676
Anatoly Kochnev watched the island's
walrus haul-outs, day after day.

79
00:05:50,789 --> 00:05:53,619
Thousands of walruses
crowded onto the beaches.

80
00:05:54,389 --> 00:05:57,919
And the bears came too,
sometimes dozens of them.

81
00:05:58,669 --> 00:06:02,713
When the sea ice drifted away in
autumn, many bears were left on the

82
00:06:02,763 --> 00:06:04,629
island with little else to eat.

83
00:06:05,708 --> 00:06:08,658
The bears did not attack
the herd from the front.

84
00:06:09,368 --> 00:06:11,388
They walked along its edge, watching.

85
00:06:12,178 --> 00:06:14,728
Some lay down near the walruses for hours,

86
00:06:14,778 --> 00:06:17,068
as if they were waiting for a signal.

87
00:06:18,157 --> 00:06:21,537
They were waiting for one thing:
the moment the herd would panic.

88
00:06:29,081 --> 00:06:33,631
When a bear comes too close,
an adult walrus does not run.

89
00:06:34,421 --> 00:06:36,121
It turns to face it.

90
00:06:36,801 --> 00:06:38,973
Its size, which makes it slow

91
00:06:39,023 --> 00:06:42,701
on land, suddenly becomes
its greatest strength.

92
00:06:43,865 --> 00:06:49,295
It raises its head, opens its
mouth, and swings its tusks.

93
00:06:50,412 --> 00:06:54,592
A single strike can drive a tusk
deep into a bear's neck or shoulder.

94
00:06:55,202 --> 00:06:59,822
And unlike a bear, the walrus
does not need to win the fight.

95
00:07:00,552 --> 00:07:02,902
It only needs to make
the attacker give up.

96
00:07:03,827 --> 00:07:08,914
Polar bears have been seriously
injured, and even killed, by walruses

97
00:07:08,964 --> 00:07:10,527
defending themselves.

98
00:07:11,662 --> 00:07:15,712
On Wrangel Island, the
observers counted the attacks.

99
00:07:16,472 --> 00:07:21,332
Every approach, every
charge, every retreat.

100
00:07:22,340 --> 00:07:25,610
Eleven of them were
attacks on adult walruses.

101
00:07:26,380 --> 00:07:31,810
Bears charged, circled and tested
them, sometimes again and again.

102
00:07:32,889 --> 00:07:36,129
Not one of those adults was killed.

103
00:07:39,137 --> 00:07:40,697
The bears seemed to know it.

104
00:07:41,367 --> 00:07:46,100
Most of them avoided adult walruses
altogether, and turned their attention

105
00:07:46,150 --> 00:07:47,507
to something smaller.

106
00:07:53,047 --> 00:07:55,067
So the bears use another strategy.

107
00:07:55,857 --> 00:07:57,727
They do not attack the walrus.

108
00:07:58,487 --> 00:07:59,907
They attack the crowd.

109
00:08:00,913 --> 00:08:02,643
A bear runs toward the herd.

110
00:08:03,393 --> 00:08:06,499
Panic spreads, and
thousands of animals rush

111
00:08:06,549 --> 00:08:08,773
for the water at the same time.

112
00:08:09,533 --> 00:08:11,610
Walruses that weigh more than a tonne

113
00:08:11,660 --> 00:08:13,903
climb over each other to reach the sea.

114
00:08:14,976 --> 00:08:20,136
In the chaos, the smallest animals
are knocked over, pinned and crushed.

115
00:08:20,866 --> 00:08:25,612
Young walruses, separated from their
mothers, are caught under animals

116
00:08:25,662 --> 00:08:27,306
many times their weight.

117
00:08:28,221 --> 00:08:30,221
That is when the bears move in.

118
00:08:30,981 --> 00:08:34,691
Not on the giants, but on the calves.

119
00:08:35,819 --> 00:08:38,149
In one season of observations, the

120
00:08:38,199 --> 00:08:42,049
scientists recorded about
twenty-five hunting attempts.

121
00:08:42,649 --> 00:08:44,459
Only two ended in a kill.

122
00:08:45,199 --> 00:08:46,459
Both were calves.

123
00:08:47,576 --> 00:08:51,016
Far more walruses died in
the stampedes themselves.

124
00:08:52,124 --> 00:08:56,361
Of the walrus carcasses examined
on the island's beaches, around

125
00:08:56,411 --> 00:08:58,554
forty-three percent were calves.

126
00:08:59,677 --> 00:09:02,897
For the bear, the stampede
is a weapon of its own.

127
00:09:03,577 --> 00:09:05,727
But it is not the only one.

128
00:09:13,355 --> 00:09:16,615
But some stories describe
something stranger.

129
00:09:17,615 --> 00:09:23,631
In 2021, three of the most experienced
polar bear scientists in the world,

130
00:09:23,681 --> 00:09:27,156
Ian Stirling, Kristin
Laidre and Erik Born,

131
00:09:27,206 --> 00:09:30,485
published a study in the journal Arctic.

132
00:09:31,522 --> 00:09:36,033
They gathered Inuit accounts and
historical reports from Greenland and the

133
00:09:36,083 --> 00:09:39,042
Canadian Arctic, going
back more than a century.

134
00:09:39,802 --> 00:09:45,363
Some came from hunters, some from
explorers, some from naturalists who had

135
00:09:45,413 --> 00:09:47,612
spent their lives on the ice.

136
00:09:48,608 --> 00:09:53,749
Again and again, the stories described
the same scene: a bear picking up a

137
00:09:53,799 --> 00:09:58,358
block of ice or a stone, and
throwing it at the head of a walrus.

138
00:09:59,479 --> 00:10:01,559
The target is always the same.

139
00:10:02,319 --> 00:10:04,229
The head.

140
00:10:04,989 --> 00:10:07,919
It is where a stunned walrus
can no longer fight back.

141
00:10:08,699 --> 00:10:13,109
Elsewhere, the walrus is protected
by skin, blubber and muscle.

142
00:10:13,839 --> 00:10:17,739
The skull is the one place where
a heavy blow can do real damage.

143
00:10:18,804 --> 00:10:23,966
The scientists also pointed to a young
male polar bear named GoGo, at a

144
00:10:24,016 --> 00:10:25,484
zoo in Osaka, Japan.

145
00:10:26,570 --> 00:10:31,369
Faced with food hung out of his reach,
GoGo threw sticks and other objects

146
00:10:31,419 --> 00:10:32,730
at it until it fell.

147
00:10:33,790 --> 00:10:36,560
Using a tool is rare in the animal world.

148
00:10:37,320 --> 00:10:42,481
Chimpanzees fish for termites with
sticks, sea otters crack shells on

149
00:10:42,531 --> 00:10:45,930
stones, and some crows
bend twigs into hooks.

150
00:10:46,700 --> 00:10:50,380
Among large predators,
it is almost unheard of.

151
00:10:51,464 --> 00:10:56,634
The authors concluded that tool use
by wild polar bears is probably rare.

152
00:10:57,314 --> 00:11:02,271
But it is likely real, and it seems
to appear in one situation above all:

153
00:11:02,321 --> 00:11:04,104
when the prey is a walrus.

154
00:11:05,196 --> 00:11:07,536
Not every scientist agrees.

155
00:11:08,326 --> 00:11:13,306
Some argue that stories, however
many, are not enough evidence.

156
00:11:14,411 --> 00:11:18,161
But if the accounts are right, the
polar bear is one of very few mammals

157
00:11:18,211 --> 00:11:19,531
that uses a tool to hunt.

158
00:11:27,096 --> 00:11:29,683
To understand why a walrus is worth that

159
00:11:29,733 --> 00:11:32,436
risk, follow it to the bottom of the sea.

160
00:11:33,528 --> 00:11:39,378
A walrus feeds in the dark, in mud and
silt, often dozens of metres down.

161
00:11:40,108 --> 00:11:42,374
It can stay underwater for up to half

162
00:11:42,424 --> 00:11:45,178
an hour, searching the
bottom with its face.

163
00:11:46,226 --> 00:11:48,456
It finds its food by touch.

164
00:11:49,066 --> 00:11:53,792
Its snout carries hundreds of thick,
sensitive whiskers, able to feel the

165
00:11:53,842 --> 00:11:56,066
shape of a clam buried in the mud.

166
00:11:57,147 --> 00:11:59,407
Then it uses its tongue like a piston.

167
00:12:00,157 --> 00:12:04,746
It pulls it back sharply, creating a
powerful suction that pulls the clam

168
00:12:04,796 --> 00:12:06,257
right out of its shell.

169
00:12:07,255 --> 00:12:09,216
A walrus can also blow jets of

170
00:12:09,266 --> 00:12:12,215
water into the seafloor
to uncover its prey.

171
00:12:13,280 --> 00:12:15,674
In a single feeding trip, one walrus

172
00:12:15,724 --> 00:12:18,780
can eat three thousand
to six thousand clams.

173
00:12:19,540 --> 00:12:21,310
It does not crush the shells.

174
00:12:22,040 --> 00:12:26,010
It leaves them almost untouched
on the seafloor, emptied.

175
00:12:27,054 --> 00:12:28,944
All that food becomes blubber.

176
00:12:29,704 --> 00:12:32,754
And blubber is exactly
what a polar bear needs.

177
00:12:39,375 --> 00:12:44,678
A walrus calf is born on the ice in
spring, and it stays with its mother

178
00:12:44,728 --> 00:12:46,215
for about two years.

179
00:12:46,955 --> 00:12:49,083
It drinks her milk for more than a

180
00:12:49,133 --> 00:12:51,695
year, long after it has learned to swim.

181
00:12:52,771 --> 00:12:57,648
When danger comes, the mother dives
with her calf, holds it against her

182
00:12:57,698 --> 00:13:00,821
body, or carries it on
her back in the water.

183
00:13:01,907 --> 00:13:04,110
Walrus mothers have been seen placing

184
00:13:04,160 --> 00:13:06,657
themselves between their calf and a bear.

185
00:13:07,337 --> 00:13:10,167
Other females in the herd sometimes help,

186
00:13:10,217 --> 00:13:13,377
forming a living barrier
of bodies and tusks.

187
00:13:14,465 --> 00:13:17,655
And that is why a
stampede is so dangerous.

188
00:13:18,385 --> 00:13:21,319
In the panic, a mother can lose her calf,

189
00:13:21,369 --> 00:13:24,645
and the calf can be
trampled by its own herd.

190
00:13:32,141 --> 00:13:34,371
Walruses are not only fighters.

191
00:13:35,171 --> 00:13:37,491
They are also surprisingly musical.

192
00:13:38,201 --> 00:13:40,461
Much of their life happens in sound.

193
00:13:41,550 --> 00:13:45,138
In winter, males sing
underwater for hours,

194
00:13:45,188 --> 00:13:48,910
with knocks, taps and
long, bell-like tones.

195
00:13:49,620 --> 00:13:52,200
The sound can carry far
through the cold water.

196
00:13:53,307 --> 00:13:58,708
Each male's song is his own, repeated
again and again to attract females

197
00:13:58,758 --> 00:14:00,197
and to warn rivals.

198
00:14:01,294 --> 00:14:05,144
On land, walruses snort, roar and grumble.

199
00:14:05,794 --> 00:14:09,214
A mother and her calf can
recognise each other's voices.

200
00:14:10,321 --> 00:14:12,568
It is a society built on sound

201
00:14:12,618 --> 00:14:16,141
and touch, packed tightly
together on the ice.

202
00:14:22,721 --> 00:14:26,311
For thousands of years,
this system worked.

203
00:14:27,426 --> 00:14:32,397
In summer, the ice edge retreated
north, but it stayed above the shallow

204
00:14:32,447 --> 00:14:35,117
shelf, where walruses could rest on the

205
00:14:35,167 --> 00:14:38,026
ice and dive straight down to their food.

206
00:14:39,109 --> 00:14:45,114
Now, in many summers, the ice melts
back past the edge of the shelf, over

207
00:14:45,164 --> 00:14:48,399
water that is thousands of metres deep.

208
00:14:49,325 --> 00:14:51,685
A walrus cannot feed that deep.

209
00:14:52,415 --> 00:14:57,185
It has to choose: stay on the ice
and go hungry, or swim to the coast.

210
00:14:57,825 --> 00:15:02,463
Every trip between the coast and the
feeding grounds costs energy, and

211
00:15:02,513 --> 00:15:04,455
calves pay the highest price.

212
00:15:05,547 --> 00:15:11,437
Since 2007, the summer ice has retreated
beyond the shelf in most years.

213
00:15:12,197 --> 00:15:14,727
What was once rare has become normal.

214
00:15:15,738 --> 00:15:20,819
And so tens of thousands of walruses
now come ashore in Alaska and Russia,

215
00:15:20,869 --> 00:15:23,018
often far from where they feed.

216
00:15:30,492 --> 00:15:36,008
In September 2014, near the village
of Point Lay, Alaska, about

217
00:15:36,058 --> 00:15:41,182
thirty-five thousand walruses
crowded onto a single beach.

218
00:15:42,295 --> 00:15:44,613
Crowds like this are exactly what the

219
00:15:44,663 --> 00:15:47,415
bears of Wrangel Island
learned to exploit.

220
00:15:48,509 --> 00:15:54,297
In 1991, a single stampede on Wrangel
Island left one hundred and four

221
00:15:54,347 --> 00:15:56,599
walruses trampled to death.

222
00:15:57,469 --> 00:15:58,829
Most of them were young.

223
00:15:59,753 --> 00:16:05,311
Today, a stampede can be started by a
bear, by a low-flying aircraft, by a

224
00:16:05,361 --> 00:16:08,923
boat, or even by a person
walking on the beach.

225
00:16:10,010 --> 00:16:15,632
In 2009, at Icy Cape in Alaska,
scientists found more than one hundred

226
00:16:15,682 --> 00:16:19,490
young walruses crushed
after a single stampede.

227
00:16:20,558 --> 00:16:25,595
Villages near the haul-outs now ask
pilots to fly high, and visitors to

228
00:16:25,645 --> 00:16:27,078
keep their distance.

229
00:16:27,798 --> 00:16:31,768
Some communities even close their
beaches during the haul-out season.

230
00:16:32,860 --> 00:16:34,660
And the bears are still there.

231
00:16:35,340 --> 00:16:39,670
Pushed ashore by the same melting
ice, they walk the beaches, hungry.

232
00:16:40,784 --> 00:16:43,694
Some of them may still
pick up a block of ice.

233
00:16:51,267 --> 00:16:53,449
Today there are an estimated two

234
00:16:53,499 --> 00:16:56,567
hundred and fifty thousand
Pacific walruses.

235
00:16:57,663 --> 00:17:01,433
The species is listed as
Vulnerable by the IUCN.

236
00:17:02,560 --> 00:17:07,623
Scientists now count walruses from
the air with drones and aircraft, and

237
00:17:07,673 --> 00:17:10,889
follow them with satellite
tags, to understand

238
00:17:10,939 --> 00:17:13,780
how they are adapting to a changing sea.

239
00:17:14,739 --> 00:17:18,976
Their future depends on the sea ice,
and on how much of it is left over

240
00:17:19,026 --> 00:17:21,019
the shallow seas where they feed.

241
00:17:21,759 --> 00:17:24,339
The polar bear depends on that ice too.

242
00:17:25,009 --> 00:17:29,529
Without it, both animals are pushed
onto the same narrow strip of land.

243
00:17:30,452 --> 00:17:35,226
We often imagine the Arctic as a
place of simple strength, where the

244
00:17:35,276 --> 00:17:37,192
biggest animal always wins.

245
00:17:38,288 --> 00:17:40,578
But the walrus shows us
a different picture.

246
00:17:41,697 --> 00:17:47,268
Here, the largest predator on land
sometimes needs patience, a crowd in

247
00:17:47,318 --> 00:17:49,297
panic, or a block of ice.

248
00:17:50,077 --> 00:17:51,347
It needs a plan.

249
00:17:52,424 --> 00:17:54,254
Not because the bear is weak.

250
00:17:54,924 --> 00:17:57,354
But because the walrus is that strong.

251
00:18:01,318 --> 00:18:03,851
If you enjoyed this film, subscribe to

252
00:18:03,901 --> 00:18:07,028
Naturexplore for a new
documentary every week.

253
00:18:07,708 --> 00:18:10,718
And tell us in the comments:
would you have guessed the rock?

